From bf5e1ea5a1b71ff611a993c0ecdb1fef10456ad1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 04:29:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/32] Fix uninitialized variable in cheap inlining of
 recursive functions

Backport of upstream fbca293 (drmortalwombat, 2026-10-18), without its
two CHECK_FUNC debug-name changes.

InterInstruction's constructor left mRemove unset. CheapInlining
inserts a new IC_PUSH_FRAME and a new IC_POP_FRAME around an inlined
call whose callee has a common frame, then turns every instruction of
the block with mRemove set into IC_NONE. It clears the flag only on the
instructions that were there before, so whether the two new frame
instructions survive depended on what the heap block held before:
either one, or both, could disappear. Losing the push while keeping
the pop raises the stack pointer by the frame size on every call;
losing the push in a caller with no frame of its own lets the callee's
stack arguments overwrite the caller's locals.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 oscar64/InterCode.cpp | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/oscar64/InterCode.cpp b/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
index 7469e83..01b43e3 100644
--- a/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
+++ b/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
@@ -4518,6 +4518,7 @@ InterInstruction::InterInstruction(const Location& loc, InterCode code)
 	mAliasing = false;
 	mMemmap = false;
 	mLockOrder = false;
+	mRemove = false;
 }
 
 void InterInstruction::Reset(void)
